# engine-strict is deliberately NOT set here. It looks like the right way to
# enforce `engines.node`, and it does enforce it — but in pnpm the same flag also
# hard-enforces every package's `os`/`cpu`/`libc` fields, so an optional
# platform binary that is perfectly correct to skip becomes a fatal install
# error on the other platform. Measured: with engine-strict=true, CI (linux/x64)
# failed every Node-installing job in ~20s with
#   ERR_PNPM_UNSUPPORTED_PLATFORM Unsupported platform for
#   @rolldown/binding-darwin-arm64@1.2.4: wanted {"os":["darwin"],"cpu":["arm64"]}
# because the lockfile carries the darwin/arm64 rolldown binding for local dev.
#
# The runtime pin is enforced instead by the `preinstall` guard in package.json
# (scripts/assert-pinned-runtime.mjs), which checks exactly the thing we care
# about — Node/ICU/Unicode against .nvmrc — and nothing about platforms.
